# The branch history whose run records the retention purge spares (keep
# rule 5 in scripts/purge-workflow-runs.sh, #874). It lives in a file of
# its own so the workflow cannot restate it, and so check-branch-refs.sh
# can require every word here to name a branch that exists.
#
# The purge still accepts GATE_BRANCHES / GATE_BRANCH_COMMITS from the
# environment, because its self-test drives those seams. Production sets
# neither.
#
# THIS FILE IS SOURCED BY THE PURGE, so it may contain comments and
# these two assignments and NOTHING ELSE. Any other line runs as its
# configuration, and it deletes run records: measured 2026-08-28,
# a line reading `DRY_RUN=0` here turned the purge's dry run into real
# deletions, and `KEEP_OPEN_PR_HEADS=0` disarmed the keep rule #740 and
# #837 were paid for -- both silently, both exiting 0. The purge now
# refuses on any other content, and its self-test drives it. Do not put a
# convenience setting in this file; it is a scope, not a config.

# Branches whose recent history must stay evidenced.
# AT LEAST ONE WORD, and the purge COUNTS the words rather than testing
# for a non-empty string. An empty value here disables keep rule 5 -- the
# purge prints "rule DISABLED" -- and before 2026-08-28 its self-test
# stayed fully green while it did. The first repair tested `-n`, which is one
# character to the side: `"   "` is non-empty and still yields zero
# branches to the loop that consumes it, so the purge deleted the commits
# it was meant to spare and printed a count of zero as success. Emptiness
# is a self-test seam, driven through the environment, never a value here.
#
# One assignment per key. A second one is legal shell and last-wins, and
# the purge refuses it: a duplicate is the second enumeration this file
# exists to remove, arriving inside the file.
#
# A WORD MAY BE A PATTERN, matched the way the workflow `branches:` filters
# match it -- `*` stops at `/`, `**` does not -- by scripts/branch-glob.sh,
# the one matcher every reader of this value shares. NOT the shell's
# globbing: an unquoted word in a `for` loop expands against the WORKING
# DIRECTORY before it splits, so the verdict becomes a function of where
# the gate was run from -- and in the direction that hurts, since a cwd
# holding a match drops the pattern-must-match-a-branch obligation without
# saying anything.
#
# THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO COUNT THE SPLITTING SITES, and the count was
# wrong. It said four, all guarded, at a moment when check-branch-refs.sh
# had just been added, split this value with expansion on, and failed from
# any directory holding a file named `2.zzz-not-a-branch`. A count in prose
# cannot notice a fifth site; it is the enumeration this file exists to
# remove, in the file itself.
#
# So the claim here is a PROPERTY and it has an instrument:
# scripts/test-scope-splitting-sites.sh discovers every script that reads
# this value, runs each one from an empty directory and from one seeded
# with file names this scope's own patterns match, and requires the two
# verdicts to be identical. A reader it cannot drive is a red case, not a
# skip -- so the population is discovered rather than listed, and a new
# reader arrives as a failure instead of as silence.
#
# `2.*` WAS HERE while the 2.x line ran on its own integration branch,
# which was renamed once per milestone (`2.x-beta`, `2.0.0-alpha.1`,
# `2.0.0`; D27: the branch follows the milestone it works toward). v2.0.0
# shipped on 2026-09-11, `dev` was fast-forwarded to it and the branch was
# deleted, so the pattern matched nothing and this scope named a branch
# that no longer exists. Development is on `dev` again. A
# future line that takes its own branch adds its pattern back here in the
# same change that creates the branch.
#
# A PATTERN THAT MATCHES NOTHING REFUSES, in the purge and in
# check-branch-refs.sh. A word that selects an empty set is how a scope
# silently shrinks, and shrinking is the direction that deletes evidence.
# That refusal is what removed the entry above rather than leaving it.
GATE_SCOPE_BRANCHES="dev main"

# How far back on each branch. 15 covers a merge burst comfortably: the
# 2026-08-13 incident put six runs on dev in twenty seconds, and a commit
# stays in range for well over a day of normal traffic. Raising it widens
# the purge's keep set, in the safe direction. A positive integer; it goes
# onto the API query as per_page, and the purge refuses `0`, a non-number, or a value carrying a stray
# character from CRLF line endings.
GATE_SCOPE_COMMITS=15
